  "grammarExplanation": "'Hay' (from 'haber') expresses the EXISTENCE of something indefinite: Hay un problema = There is a problem. It has only one form for singular and plural. Use it when the thing isn't specific or known yet. 'Estar' tells you WHERE a specific thing or person is: El libro está en la mesa = The book is on the table (we know which book). A good trick: if you can name or point at it, it's probably 'está'. Also learn these two extras:\n\n• Había = there was / there were (past existence)\n• Hay que + infinitive = one has to do something (obligation): Hay que madrugar.\n\nConjugation of estar for location:\n• Está = he/she/it is • Están = they are",
  "vocabulary": {
    "hay": {
      "mnemonic": "'Eye' — with your eye you spot what exists.",
      "explanation": "Impersonal form of haber: there is / there are."
    },
    "había": {
      "mnemonic": "Había happened in the past.",
      "explanation": "Past (imperfect) of hay: there was / there were."
    },
    "madrugar": {
      "mnemonic": "Mad-rush in the morning.",
      "explanation": "To get up very early."
    },
    "nevera": {
      "mnemonic": "Never a warm drink in the nevera.",
      "explanation": "Fridge (Peninsular Spanish)."
    },
    "cajón": {
      "mnemonic": "A cajón is a big box you draw open.",
      "explanation": "Drawer."
    },
    "esquina": {
      "mnemonic": "Around the esquina, the corner shop.",
      "explanation": "Corner."
    },
    "lleno": {
      "mnemonic": "Lleno = full to the brim.",
      "explanation": "Full."
    },
    "tráfico": {
      "mnemonic": "Traffic jam on the way to traffic-oh.",
